From: =?UTF-8?q?Berkay=20K=C3=B6sebay?= <brkosebay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 16:52:29 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: apple_tristar_charger: Add the Lightning
 ID-bus master

A Lightning accessory announces itself on the ID bus: asked for its
digital ID, it answers with six bytes that say what it is and which pins
it uses. The Tristar (CBTL1610) is the bus master. The host loads a
request, reversed, into the TX window that ends at register 0x60, reads
it back, starts the transfer through 0x61 and polls the event register
0x0d; the Tristar clocks the wire, frames BREAKs, appends the request
CRC and leaves the response in the RX window at 0x20.

Add the master as the commands iOS sends, in its order and with its I2C
transaction shapes: GetID (0x74) after acknowledging a latched pin fault
and releasing a latched ID-bus reset hold; for the camera adapter the
follow-up iOS runs before USB host mode, the interface information
(0x76), the module and accessory serials (0x78, 0x7a) and two SystemInfo
packets (0x84) that the adapter normally leaves unanswered, where iOS's
timeout recovery (engine abort, BREAK before the next transfer) applies;
and the power handshake, GetState (0x72) then the power request (0x70).
Serial numbers read from the accessory are not kept.

The ID-bus master is a separate object of the charger module, under the
new APPLE_TRISTAR_ACCESSORY option. The module keeps its name; only the
composite object is spelled with dashes, so that the charger's source
file keeps its name.

Tested on an iPad 5 (J71t) with the Lightning to USB camera adapter.

[Orchard: from orchard PR #126, commit 864b4971; commit message only]

Assisted-by: LLM
